{"stem":"","quantityA":"$\dfrac{7}{8} \div \dfrac{3}{5}$","quantityB":"$1.45$","relation":"A","checkA":"(7/8)/(3/5)","checkB":"1.45","explanation":"Dividing by $\frac{3}{5}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\frac{5}{3}$: $\frac{7}{8} \div \frac{3}{5} = \frac{7}{8} \times \frac{5}{3} = \frac{35}{24}$.

To compare $\frac{35}{24}$ with $1.45 = \frac{29}{20}$, put both over a common denominator (120): $\frac{35}{24} = \frac{175}{120}$ and $\frac{29}{20} = \frac{174}{120}$. Since $175 > 174$, Quantity A is greater -- but only by $\frac{1}{120}$, so the two values are very close (about $1.4583$ versus $1.45$).

The trap is rounding too early: if you convert $\frac{7}{8}=0.875$ and estimate $0.875 \div 0.6$ as "about $1.45$," the values look equal and you'd miss the small but real difference. Carrying the division (or working in exact fractions, as above) to enough precision is what settles the comparison. A second common slip is inverting the wrong fraction -- computing $\frac{3}{5}\div\frac{7}{8}=\frac{24}{35}\approx0.686$ -- which would wrongly suggest B is greater.","difficulty":3}

{"stem":"When a positive integer $n$ is divided by $8$, the remainder is $3$. When the same integer $n$ is divided by $12$, the remainder is $7$. What is the remainder when $n$ is divided by $24$?","correctValue":19,"answerIsFraction":false,"checkExpr":"19","explanation":"Since $24$ is the least common multiple of $8$ and $12$, the remainder of $n$ upon division by $24$ is uniquely determined by the two given conditions -- but only if the conditions are consistent with each other, which is worth checking first. From $n\equiv 3\pmod 8$, $n$ is $3$ more than a multiple of $4$, so $n\equiv 3\pmod 4$. From $n\equiv 7\pmod{12}$, $n$ is $7$ more than a multiple of $4$, so $n\equiv 3\pmod 4$ as well -- the two conditions agree mod $4$, so a solution mod $24$ exists.

To find it, list numbers that are $3$ more than a multiple of $8$: $3, 11, 19, 27, 35, \ldots$. Check each against "remainder $7$ when divided by $12$": $19 = 12(1)+7$ works. Since $24$ is the LCM, this residue repeats every $24$, so $n \equiv 19 \pmod{24}$ is the unique answer.

A common error is to combine the remainders naively -- for instance, adding them ($3+7=10$) or taking their difference ($7-3=4$) -- rather than actually searching for the integer that simultaneously satisfies both conditions; neither shortcut is valid here. Another trap is stopping at the first shared multiple check without verifying that $8$ and $12$'s remainder conditions are even compatible (they could have clashed mod $\gcd(8,12)=4$, which would mean no such $n$ exists at all).","difficulty":3}
